`RelayApplication(dataDir, bind, port, env)` → uses webSocketPort = port+1. So management and WSS are always on different ports in this implementation. Interesting — so how does the real Host derive the WSS URL from management? `deriveWebSocketUrl(managementUrl)` → ws://host:port/relay/v1 where port = management port. In production, Caddy maps HTTPS 443 → management 127.0.0.1:18090 and also /relay/v1 → the websocket port? Let's check: relay WebSocketServer listens on port+1 = 18091 presumably, and Caddy proxies /relay/v1 (the path) to... Hmm.

Actually the WSS URL derived is `new URL("/relay/v1", managementUrl)` → same origin, path /relay/v1. In production behind Caddy, https://ji.lilypad.com.cn/relay/v1 → Caddy routes to the WSS backend port. So in production the derived URL works because a reverse proxy unifies them. The fixture has no proxy for the raw port.

So to test a live connection in the fixture, I need a proxy that maps management origin: /relay/v1 → WSS backend, everything else → management backend. I can write one in Node! A simple TCP/HTTP proxy:
- HTTP server on port P: for requests with path starting /relay/v1 and Upgrade: websocket → proxy the raw socket to the WSS port; else forward to management port.

Actually simpler: a raw TCP router on port P that inspects the first bytes... messy. Better: use Node's http server with 'upgrade' event handler: for Upgrade requests, net.connect(wssPort) and pipe; for regular requests, proxy via fetch/http.request to management port. That's ~50 lines.

Then:
- Host managementUrl = http://127.0.0.1:P/ (loopback → allowed as plaintext).
- Derived WSS = ws://127.0.0.1:P/relay/v1 → hits my proxy → forwards to fixture WSS port. 

That makes the whole flow real: the Host connects through the same origin it derived, exactly like production behind Caddy.

Also then relayPresent via proxy works (management path forwarded).

Hmm, but there's the CORS/allowed-origin: fixture relay has JI_RELAY_ALLOWED_ORIGIN = webOrigin. My Node proxy requests to management won't carry Origin. Fine.

Also: does the fixture's WSS server require the Host to present an access token issued by the fixture's management? Yes: the Host got its access token by calling POST /api/v1/hosts/{hostId}/access on the management URL (through my proxy) with the owner bearer. The token is issued by the fixture's accessSessions — same process serving WSS. 

Let me implement the proxy in Node:

```js
function startRelayGateway(managementPort, webSocketPort) {
  const server = createHttpServer((req, res) => {
    const proxy = httpRequest({host:'127.0.0.1', port: managementPort, path: req.url, method: req.method, headers: req.headers}, upstream => {
      res.writeHead(upstream.statusCode, upstream.headers)
      upstream.pipe(res)
    })
    req.pipe(proxy)
  })
  server.on('upgrade', (req, socket, head) => {
    const upstream = netConnect({host:'127.0.0.1', port: webSocketPort})
    upstream.on('connect', () => {
      upstream.write(`${req.method} ${req.url} HTTP/1.1\r\n` + rawHeaders... )
    })
  })
}
```

For the upgrade path, rebuilding the raw request headers is easier with `req.rawHeaders`. Let me write:

```js
server.on("upgrade", (req, socket, head) => {
  const upstream = net.connect({host: "127.0.0.1", port: webSocketPort}, () => {
    let raw = `${req.method} ${req.url} HTTP/1.1\r\n`
    for (let i = 0; i < req.rawHeaders.length; i += 2) raw += `${req.rawHeaders[i]}: ${req.rawHeaders[i+1]}\r\n`
    raw += "\r\n"
    upstream.write(raw)
    if (head?.length) upstream.write(head)
    upstream.pipe(socket); socket.pipe(upstream)
  })
  upstream.on("error", () => socket.destroy())
  socket.on("error", () => upstream.destroy())
})
```

For the HTTP proxy, need to strip hop-by-hop? Keep simple: forward method, path, headers (drop host? set host to 127.0.0.1:managementPort). Node's http.request with headers including 'host' from client (127.0.0.1:P) — the relay's management handlers may not check Host. Safer to set host header to `127.0.0.1:${managementPort}`.

